<header class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow">Understand official hail reports</div><h1>Hail Size Guide</h1><p>Common object comparisons make hail diameter easier to picture, but size alone cannot determine whether a particular vehicle was damaged.</p></header>
<article class="section narrow prose">
<h2>How hail diameter is reported</h2>
<p>Official hail observations generally describe the estimated diameter of a hailstone at a particular place and time. National Weather Service warnings, trained spotter reports, emergency-management observations, and preliminary Storm Prediction Center reports may use inches or familiar objects. The comparison is approximate: a storm can produce stones of different sizes, and an observer may report the largest stone available rather than an average across the storm.</p>
<p>Diameter is measured across the widest part of the stone. It is not a damage rating, a measurement of dent depth, or proof that every location beneath a storm received the same hail. Reports also vary in precision. Some come from trained observers with direct measurements; others use reasonable visual comparisons under difficult conditions.</p>
<h2>Common hail-size comparisons</h2>
<div class="grid">{sizes.map(([name,size,note])=><section class="card"><h3>{name}</h3><p><strong>{size}</strong></p><p>{note}</p></section>)}</div>
<h2>What size hail can damage a vehicle?</h2>
<p><strong>There is no single guaranteed damage threshold.</strong> Even relatively small hail can cause damage under the right conditions, while larger hail generally increases damage risk. A size in an official report describes an observed stone, not the outcome for every exposed vehicle nearby.</p>
<p>Wind speed and direction matter because wind-driven hail can strike doors, fenders, quarter panels, and glass at an angle instead of falling mainly onto horizontal surfaces. A longer storm or a dense concentration of stones creates more impacts. A moving vehicle may meet hail at a different relative speed and angle than a parked vehicle. Cover from a building, tree, carport, or another vehicle can also change exposure within a very small area.</p>
<h2>The vehicle changes the outcome</h2>
<p>Panel orientation, shape, bracing, and material influence how metal responds. A broad roof or hood surface behaves differently from a curved body line or an area supported by reinforcement. Steel and aluminum panels have different working characteristics, but neither material makes a vehicle immune to hail. Existing paint condition, prior body repairs, refinishing, and previous dents can also affect what an inspection finds and whether Paintless Dent Repair is appropriate.</p>
<p>A shallow dent may be hard to see in flat daylight. Professional PDR lighting creates controlled reflections across a panel, making subtle lows and interrupted reflection lines easier to identify. That is why an official report or a quick outdoor look should not replace a vehicle-specific inspection.</p>
<h2>Northern Colorado historical context</h2>
<p>The verified Peak Country archive contains <strong>{summary.overall.report_count.toLocaleString()} NOAA/NCEI hail observations across {summary.overall.hail_day_count} hail days</strong> within 50 statute miles of the Greeley geographic anchor for 2016–2025. The largest supplied magnitude in that completed dataset is <strong>{summary.overall.largest_reported_hail_inches.toFixed(2)} inches</strong>. Those figures are derived from the published dataset, but they still do not prove damage to any individual vehicle.</p>
<h2>Why object comparisons need care</h2><p>Common names are useful shorthand, but they should not be treated as exact measurements unless the observer supplied a measured diameter. A stone can be irregular rather than perfectly round, can melt before it is examined, and can differ from nearby stones in the same storm. When comparing a report with a vehicle, keep the official time, location, source, and stated precision together instead of relying on the object name alone.</p>
<h2>Use reports as context, then inspect the vehicle</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/hail-tracker/">Live Hail Tracker</a> for current official-source context and the <a href="/northern-colorado-hail-history/">Northern Colorado Hail History</a> for completed historical records. After conditions are safe, follow the <a href="/after-a-hailstorm/">steps to check a vehicle after a hailstorm</a>. If you see dents or remain unsure, learn about <a href="/auto-hail-repair/">auto hail repair</a> or <a href="/free-hail-inspection/">request a free hail inspection</a>.</p>
